{
  "title": "Orphaned application data",
  "_comment": "Config for detecting leftover data from apps that are no longer installed. The tool scans each location for reverse-DNS (bundle-id) named folders whose bundle id is NOT registered with LaunchServices and is NOT covered by skipPrefixes/sharedComponents, then offers to move them to the Trash. 'strip' removes a filename suffix before treating a name as a bundle id. 'sharedComponents' are prefix-matched: they name updaters, crash/analytics SDKs and security agents that legitimately live on disk without an app of their own bundle id being installed — without them they look exactly like orphans. 'apps' covers what the bundle-id scan cannot see: data an app keeps under a name that is not its bundle id (VS Code's '~/Library/Application Support/Code' and '~/.vscode'). Each entry is an app's bundle 'id', a display 'name' and '~/' 'paths'. When no installed app owns 'id', its existing paths join the orphan row for that id. A path is literal, or ends in '<Product>[[:digit:]]*' for a folder per version (JetBrains/PyCharm2025.1 but never PyCharmCE2025.1). 'optional' paths get a row of their own, '<name> (optional)', with 'note' as its description: folders a command-line tool also uses (~/.docker, ~/.ollama, ~/.codex, ~/.android, ~/.cursor) and Docker Desktop's sandbox container, which holds its VM disk. Like every orphan row, both arrive unchecked. 'keptByCommand' names the commands that keep such a folder: while any of them is on PATH the folder is never offered, whichever app lists it (~/.docker stays while OrbStack's or Colima's docker is installed, even after Docker Desktop is gone). A path listed by several entries stays while any of them is installed (Zed and Zed Preview share '~/.config/zed'). An id may be a vendor namespace when the real one carries a version: 'com.sublimetext' stays while com.sublimetext.4 (or .3, .5) is installed. Ids and paths come from each app's Homebrew cask zap list. 'paths' holds only folders the app alone writes: never a parent shared by a vendor's other products (JetBrains, Google), and never a folder a command-line tool also uses — those go in 'optional'.",
  "locations": [
    { "path": "~/Library/Application Support" },
    { "path": "~/Library/Caches" },
    { "path": "~/Library/HTTPStorages" },
    { "path": "~/Library/WebKit" },
    { "path": "~/Library/Logs" },
    { "path": "~/Library/Preferences", "strip": ".plist" },
    { "path": "~/Library/Saved Application State", "strip": ".savedState" }
  ],
  "skipPrefixes": [
    "com.apple.",
    "group.com.apple.",
    "systemgroup.",
    "org.swift.",
    "com.apple"
  ],
